INT. WEATHER STATION, PACIFIC ATOLL - DAY

A cinderblock hut. Louvered windows. Barometers, a wall of dials, a hand-cranked radio on a rough table. Heat shimmers outside.

PETTY OFFICER ANSELM BOUCHARD, 30s, sunburnt, shirtless under a rain slicker, logs numbers in a ledger.

BOUCHARD
(muttering)
Seven-oh-three. Seven-oh-two. Seven-oh-three.

He taps the barometer. It does not move.

The radio crackles.

FENWICK (V.O.)
(radio)
Atoll Three, Atoll Three, this is Fleet Weather. Do you copy?

Bouchard picks up the handset. Takes his time.

BOUCHARD
Fleet Weather, Atoll Three. I copy.

FENWICK (V.O.)
(radio)
Anselm, is that you?

BOUCHARD
It's the only voice out here, Fenwick.

FENWICK (V.O.)
(radio)
Give me the numbers.

BOUCHARD
Seven-oh-three and steady. Wind variable, light. Seas calm. Nothing on the horizon.

FENWICK (V.O.)
(radio)
Nothing at all?

BOUCHARD
A seabird. Maybe two.

FENWICK (V.O.)
(radio)
Understood. Hold position.

BOUCHARD
We've held it eleven weeks.

FENWICK (V.O.)
(radio)
Then you're good at it. Out.

The radio clicks dead. Bouchard sets the handset in its cradle.

BOUCHARD
(to the room)
Gentlemen, the war continues without us.

No one answers. The room has no gentlemen.

He goes back to the ledger. Writes: no change. Leans back. Closes his eyes.

BOUCHARD (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Tomorrow, I swear, I'll paint the wall.

Outside, a seabird calls.
